WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.980 --> 00:00:12.300
Hey Wisecrack, Jared here.

00:00:12.300 --> 00:00:15.719
Today we’re talking about the story of a
guy who takes down the finance industry as

00:00:15.719 --> 00:00:17.370
he talks to imaginary friends.

00:00:17.370 --> 00:00:19.380
That’s right, Mr. Robot.

00:00:19.380 --> 00:00:23.430
Mr. Robot follows hoodie-clad hacker Elliot
Alderson, as he and his friends instigate

00:00:23.430 --> 00:00:28.310
a revolution and take down E Corp, a fictional
megacorporation that’s the gold-standard

00:00:28.310 --> 00:00:34.139
of corporate malevolence, a conglomeration
of Bank of America, Walmart and Enron.

00:00:34.140 --> 00:00:34.860
Also, Elliot is crazy.

00:00:38.740 --> 00:00:41.740
On the surface, Mr. Robot is a show criticizing capitalism.

00:00:46.040 --> 00:00:50.179
But to really understand Mr. Robot, we have
to dig deeper into an idea that is woven into

00:00:50.180 --> 00:00:53.360
the very fabric of the show- alienation.

00:00:53.360 --> 00:00:58.780
Alienation means to be estranged and isolated,
in the parlance of Mr. Robot: disconnected.

00:00:58.780 --> 00:01:01.480
Philosophy deals with different kinds of alienation.

00:01:01.489 --> 00:01:05.409
There’s alienation from humanity, from the
world, and even personal alienation - where

00:01:05.409 --> 00:01:09.140
you have become a stranger to yourself - Mr.
Robot explores them all.

00:01:09.140 --> 00:01:12.729
Welcome to this Wisecrack Edition on the philosophy
of “Mr Robot.”

00:01:12.729 --> 00:01:15.030
Oh, and spoiler alert.

00:01:15.030 --> 00:01:18.310
Mr. Robot is about lonely people trying
to connect.

00:01:18.310 --> 00:01:22.670
Sometimes that means quite literally connecting,
illegally, to other people's computers.

00:01:22.670 --> 00:01:28.840
Agent DiPierro has no life, and struggles
to connect with both strange men on the internet,

00:01:28.840 --> 00:01:31.780
and her Amazon Alexa.

00:01:31.780 --> 00:01:36.720
Elliot struggles to connect with his family,
his past, his friends, and even reality.

00:01:36.720 --> 00:01:40.820
And the masses, according to Elliot, can only
connect to each other with Facebook,

00:01:40.829 --> 00:01:42.229
Instagram, and LinkedIn.

00:01:42.229 --> 00:01:47.109
The show even employs visual tricks to emphasize
the distance between characters, such as wide

00:01:47.109 --> 00:01:50.649
angle lenses and framing characters near the
edge of the screen.

00:01:50.649 --> 00:01:54.961
Elliot’s inability to connect is caused
by more than just his social anxiety, or that

00:01:54.961 --> 00:01:56.640
he talks to his dead father.

00:01:56.640 --> 00:02:01.220
Instead, Elliot’s alienation has everything
to do with God.

00:02:01.220 --> 00:02:02.960
Let me explain.

00:02:02.960 --> 00:02:07.580
Elliot is unable to understand his actions,
his motives, or even his relationship to other

00:02:07.580 --> 00:02:08.200
people.

00:02:08.200 --> 00:02:12.460
Mr. Robot takes charge of Elliot's physical
body, interacting with people on his behalf,

00:02:12.470 --> 00:02:15.980
all while Elliot is completely unaware that
anything has transpired.

00:02:15.980 --> 00:02:20.090
As a result, Elliot is unable to understand
his relationship with his sister, Darlene,

00:02:20.090 --> 00:02:22.750
or anyone else he might have interacted with
as Mr. Robot.

00:02:22.750 --> 00:02:27.260
And while Elliot’s psychosis makes his alienation
from himself and others unique, the logic

00:02:27.260 --> 00:02:30.040
that dictates that psychosis is nearly universal.

00:02:32.549 --> 00:02:36.640
For German philosopher Ludwig Feuerbach, whose
name literally means “fire stream,” but

00:02:36.650 --> 00:02:41.530
I digress, God was the primary engine of alienation
during the 19th century.

00:02:41.530 --> 00:02:45.280
Humans created the figure of God, according
to Feuerbach, and instilled him with their

00:02:45.280 --> 00:02:49.940
highest ideals: justice, love, wisdom, and
so on.

00:02:49.940 --> 00:02:55.120
But being a forgetful species, they forgot,
looked at this virtuous God and thought “Hey,

00:02:55.120 --> 00:02:59.250
we’re so much like this guy, he made us
just like him.”

00:02:59.250 --> 00:03:04.750
In this way, the fact that man made God in
man’s image is inverted to “God created

00:03:04.750 --> 00:03:10.579
man in God’s image” As a result, Christianity
is alienating: we become estranged from our

00:03:10.579 --> 00:03:15.189
ideals, ourselves, and each other because
we can only relate to them through the medium

00:03:15.189 --> 00:03:18.440
of God, a figure that we’ve since lost control
of.

00:03:18.440 --> 00:03:21.390
If this seems strangely familiar, it should.

00:03:21.390 --> 00:03:25.989
Elliot creates the figure of Mr. Robot, forgets
he created him, and then goes about letting

00:03:25.989 --> 00:03:27.720
Mr. Robot run his life.

00:03:27.720 --> 00:03:32.909
In good Feuerbachian fashion, Elliot says:
“Mr. Robot has become my god…" and even

00:03:32.909 --> 00:03:35.500
creates Mr. Robot in his own crazy image.

00:03:35.500 --> 00:03:39.480
Mr. Robot is even likened to omniscient God, always watching.

00:03:43.860 --> 00:03:48.180
Elliot’s first response to the problem of
Mr. Robot is to get rid of him.

00:03:48.180 --> 00:03:52.240
He pumps himself full of Adderall and starts
journaling in the hopes that he can kill him

00:03:52.240 --> 00:03:52.900
off.

00:03:52.900 --> 00:03:56.140
This, in a way, parallels the atheist response
to God.

00:04:01.340 --> 00:04:05.260
We invented God, now we realize it, so let’s
dump God.

00:04:05.260 --> 00:04:10.010
But Elliot’s therapist convinces him the
answer isn’t to dump Mr. Robot, it’s to

00:04:10.010 --> 00:04:12.460
accept his delusion as part of him.

00:04:18.060 --> 00:04:22.780
For Feuerbach, the biggest problem isn’t that we
invented God, it’s that we inverted our

00:04:22.780 --> 00:04:26.080
relationship to God and said he created us.

00:04:30.120 --> 00:04:34.680
Feuerbach thinks that God is the ultimate
object of thinking man, a creation that speaks

00:04:34.690 --> 00:04:36.810
to the unlimited potential of our reason.

00:04:36.810 --> 00:04:42.120
Similarly, Mr. Robot represents Elliot’s
highest ideals: creating a better future without

00:04:42.120 --> 00:04:43.120
Evilcorp.

00:04:43.220 --> 00:04:47.320
One of the big issues with this kind of alienation
is the loss of responsibility - you know,

00:04:47.320 --> 00:04:49.360
the old “I don’t hate you, God does.”

00:04:50.780 --> 00:04:54.310
Elliot either suppresses the memory of the
unpleasant acts he has to commit in the name

00:04:54.310 --> 00:04:57.300
of a better future, like allegedly killing
a guy

00:04:58.420 --> 00:05:01.160
or imagines Mr. Robot is responsible.

00:05:02.040 --> 00:05:05.950
In the process, Elliot is unable to directly
relate to the world he helps shape or his

00:05:05.960 --> 00:05:09.800
peers and instead has to relate through the
medium of Mr. Robot.

00:05:10.260 --> 00:05:14.600
It’s only when Elliot admits that Mr. Robot
is his own creation, that he can finally carve

00:05:14.600 --> 00:05:16.840
a path out of his own alienation.

00:05:18.860 --> 00:05:24.260
When we’ve mixed-up the relationship between God and the world around us, the world becomes confusing.

00:05:24.260 --> 00:05:29.300
Cause and effect seem to invert themselves,
reality slips away, and we lose touch with

00:05:29.310 --> 00:05:30.470
our fellow man.

00:05:30.470 --> 00:05:35.020
No longer are we the drivers of our fate,
but events are laid out by the divine, or

00:05:35.020 --> 00:05:36.160
your imaginary friend.

00:05:36.160 --> 00:05:40.650
We are no longer the judge of our actions
or the actions of others, God is.

00:05:40.650 --> 00:05:44.660
The rest of the world doesn’t see Mr. Robot,
but that doesn’t stop the show from drawing

00:05:44.660 --> 00:05:48.220
an explicit connection between Elliot’s
delusions and theirs.

00:05:53.820 --> 00:05:59.160
While Mr. Robot may be Elliot’s God, the
masses have a different God: consumerism.

00:05:59.220 --> 00:06:02.260
Which brings us to a different kind of alienation.

00:06:02.270 --> 00:06:06.600
Show creator Sam Esmail holds nothing back
in paying homage to other films critical of

00:06:06.600 --> 00:06:07.600
consumer culture.

00:06:07.600 --> 00:06:12.040
Aside from the obvious Fight Club and V for
Vendetta references, one scene evokes John

00:06:12.040 --> 00:06:16.900
Carpenter’s “They Live”, a film exploring
the "truth" behind modern capitalism and Tyrell

00:06:16.900 --> 00:06:21.980
Wellick is one big ode to American Psycho,
a film where people become as disposable as

00:06:21.980 --> 00:06:22.980
commodities.

00:06:23.360 --> 00:06:27.890
Likewise, one of the central tenants of Mr.
Robot is that consumer culture is a collective

00:06:27.890 --> 00:06:31.840
delusion, one the estranges us from the world
and each other.

00:06:31.840 --> 00:06:35.260
When Mr. Robot reminds Elliot that nothing
is real, he says:

00:06:35.260 --> 00:06:37.500
”A world built on fantasy.

00:06:37.500 --> 00:06:40.040
Synthetic emotions in the forms of pills.

00:06:40.040 --> 00:06:43.180
Psychological warfare in the form of advertising."

00:06:43.220 --> 00:06:45.680
In season 1, Elliot decides to be "normal".

00:06:45.700 --> 00:06:48.780
by hearting things on Instagram, watching Marvel movies,

00:06:48.840 --> 00:06:50.700
and drinking Starbucks.

00:06:51.660 --> 00:06:56.420
Throughout the show, material items and wealth
estrange them from their true selves.

00:06:56.430 --> 00:07:00.960
Whenever Angela Moss is confronted about her
working for Evilcorp, for instance, she defends

00:07:00.960 --> 00:07:03.340
her character with displays of wealth.

00:07:03.340 --> 00:07:08.290
When she goes buy a new pair of shoes to replace
a pair covered in her coworkers brains, the

00:07:08.290 --> 00:07:13.780
salesman confronts her saying: “You mean
to tell me you witnessed this thing,

00:07:15.920 --> 00:07:17.880
and you're here to buy new shoes?"

00:07:17.880 --> 00:07:22.220
Angela: “I don't know who you think you're
talking to, but I'll try the Prada's next.”

00:07:22.220 --> 00:07:29.060
When again confronted at a bar by her father's
friend, she responds: "I'm 27 and I've got a

00:07:29.060 --> 00:07:31.640
six figure salary with the biggest conglomerate in history.

00:07:33.760 --> 00:07:35.220
And I'm just getting started."

00:07:35.220 --> 00:07:39.640
If Angela’s motives seem unclear to the
viewer, they’re probably also unclear to

00:07:39.640 --> 00:07:42.880
her: an estranged world is a disorienting
world.

00:07:42.880 --> 00:07:47.990
Angela, who stresses she needs the job Evilcorp
offers her, is in the same plight as the guy

00:07:47.990 --> 00:07:50.140
who has to euthanize dogs for a living.

00:07:52.400 --> 00:07:57.620
Or the homeless guy that allows Tyrell to beat him senseless while wearing sterilized surgical gloves.

00:07:57.620 --> 00:08:02.340
In prison, Leon can only relate to Elliot
with the prison’s DVD collection, namely,

00:08:02.340 --> 00:08:04.520
Seinfeld, a show about nothing.

00:08:07.160 --> 00:08:11.900
In all of these cases, mass culture and commodities
inhibit our very being.

00:08:11.920 --> 00:08:15.920
Advertising and media encourage us to destroy
a part of ourselves to fit in.

00:08:23.300 --> 00:08:27.120
It sells us prepackaged narratives about love
and life.

00:08:27.180 --> 00:08:32.360
For Elliot, only when we destroy the alienating
forces of consumerism can we finally be free.

00:08:41.480 --> 00:08:46.420
Thus, Mr. Robot shows another kind of alienation:
commodity fetishism.

00:08:46.420 --> 00:08:50.940
Commodity fetishism works a lot like Feuerbach’s
alienation, and it should: it’s creator,

00:08:50.940 --> 00:08:54.639
Karl Marx appropriated Feuerbach’s work
for his own ends.

00:08:54.639 --> 00:08:58.690
For Marx, instead of God mediating human relations,
commodities do.

00:08:58.690 --> 00:09:04.009
We imbue commodities with abstract value in
such a way that everything becomes commodified.

00:09:04.009 --> 00:09:08.769
For Tyrell Wellick, every interaction is an
exchange, even going so far as to use his

00:09:08.769 --> 00:09:11.250
own body in his pursuit of greater wealth.

00:09:11.250 --> 00:09:15.600
In the case of the Washington Township trial,
the people who died as a result of EvilCorp’s

00:09:15.600 --> 00:09:17.780
actions were treated as mere commodities,

00:09:17.780 --> 00:09:20.740
profits are more important than people's lives.

00:09:20.740 --> 00:09:25.270
It’s for this same reason that EvilCorp’s
executives can so easily forgive Colby after

00:09:25.270 --> 00:09:30.290
he testified against them, every action isn’t
some indicator of your character, but an exchange

00:09:30.290 --> 00:09:32.000
of goods and services.

00:09:32.000 --> 00:09:36.930
In the same vein, Phillip Price hires Angela, despite the fact that she blackmailed him.

00:09:36.930 --> 00:09:39.680
But what exactly are the stakes of alienation?

00:09:39.680 --> 00:09:42.139
In the world of Mr. Robot, it’s control.

00:09:42.139 --> 00:09:46.839
For both Feuerbach and Marx, the world is
the object of collective human labor, a labor

00:09:46.840 --> 00:09:51.120
that affirms our “species-being,” our
membership in the human race.

00:09:51.120 --> 00:09:55.720
Alienation destroys that species-being, because
the world that we collectively build together

00:09:55.720 --> 00:09:58.840
has now been hijacked by someone or something
else.

00:09:58.840 --> 00:10:04.810
It’s no surprise then, that every character
from Angela, to Elliot, to Tyrell, to Price

00:10:04.810 --> 00:10:07.680
and Minister Zhang are vying for control.

00:10:12.680 --> 00:10:17.520
The series even opens with a monologue about
the people who have the real control in society.

00:10:17.540 --> 00:10:21.240
Secret board meetings control who lives and
dies over shrimp cocktails.

00:10:28.640 --> 00:10:33.640
Young people are racked with debt forcing
them into jobs they hate, and Elliot can’t

00:10:33.660 --> 00:10:36.360
stop the voice in his head from taking over
his life.

00:10:36.360 --> 00:10:40.981
Minister Zhang is obsessed with time, surrounding
himself with clocks, if only to remind him

00:10:41.000 --> 00:10:43.200
of the one thing he can’t control.

00:10:58.960 --> 00:11:03.720
Elliot is seen constantly scribbling in his
notebook about control, or fighting for control

00:11:03.730 --> 00:11:04.950
with Mr. Robot.

00:11:04.960 --> 00:11:09.259
He attributes Shayla’s abuse by Fernando
Vera to the invisible hand - the economic

00:11:09.259 --> 00:11:11.360
forces that control her life.

00:11:17.240 --> 00:11:21.800
In his therapist’s office, he likens the
freedom afforded by contemporary society to

00:11:21.800 --> 00:11:23.240
the choice between Pepsi and Coke.

00:11:23.269 --> 00:11:26.550
The real question is: where’s the RC Cola?

00:11:26.550 --> 00:11:29.920
Both Tyrell and Elliot blame their
fathers for their lack of control.

00:11:29.920 --> 00:11:34.280
Early on, Elliot blames his father for giving
up when Evilcorp poisoned him.

00:11:43.280 --> 00:11:47.760
Tyrell never wants to become his father - whose
inability to speak English was a reminder

00:11:47.760 --> 00:11:49.120
of his helplessness.

00:11:55.040 --> 00:11:59.839
The journal that Elliot frantically writes
about control in is aptly named “red wheelbarrow,”

00:11:59.840 --> 00:12:02.720
Tyrell’s own reminder of his father’s
impotence.

00:12:03.840 --> 00:12:07.559
Tyrell also likens murdering Sharon Knowles
to absolute power.

00:12:07.560 --> 00:12:10.800
And he, and others, are really into playing
God.

00:12:17.360 --> 00:12:22.120
Elliot at times is unsure if he's Robot's
God, or Mr. Robot is his God.

00:12:22.120 --> 00:12:27.149
In the end, Mr. Robot shows that nobody is
really in control in an alienated world.

00:12:27.149 --> 00:12:30.600
The people at the top have become victims
of their own machinations - while they may

00:12:30.600 --> 00:12:34.610
try to create an illusion of control with
armies of lawyers, convoluted conspiracies,

00:12:34.610 --> 00:12:36.400
and bunkered data centers.

00:12:36.400 --> 00:12:40.120
They all experience the same alienating forces as the rest of society.

00:12:40.120 --> 00:12:43.200
Whether it be in the form of God or commodity fetishism.

00:12:44.040 --> 00:12:48.520
Mr. Robot is not only a show about lonely
people trying to connect, it’s about the

00:12:48.540 --> 00:12:50.970
engines that drive that loneliness.

00:12:50.970 --> 00:12:55.759
Whether it’s God, our imaginary friends,
or the things we own, that engine distorts

00:12:55.759 --> 00:12:58.500
our perceptions and turns reality on its head.

00:12:58.500 --> 00:13:02.899
We can never really be sure what’s reality
or delusion in Mr. Robot, and that’s sort

00:13:02.899 --> 00:13:03.899
of the point.

00:13:03.899 --> 00:13:09.120
In an alienated world, can we be sure any
kind of reality exists?

